// What matter is made of, as light finds it (docs/design.md, "What an author writes"): plain
// physical numbers, never a tone. A substance says how matter answers light; only the look turns
// light into what the screen shows (`picture::look`). An author gives one where they say what a
// thing is (`shape.with(substance)`, std::field's channels), and where two things are joined
// smoothly their substances blend across the join as their shapes do (`Blend`, field by field).
//
// Today a substance scatters light evenly every way (a Lambertian surface) and may give off light
// of its own. What else a substance will say (how rough or glossy, how much light passes through
// and how it bends, how dense a medium is) is added here, as more numbers, when a thing needs it;
// every member must blend field by field.

use std::field::Blend
use std::cmp::Eq
use std::hash::StateHash

/// What matter is made of here.
pub struct Substance: Copy + GpuData + Blend + Eq + StateHash {
    /// The share of the light falling on it that it scatters back, each colour (its diffuse
    /// reflectance, linear, from 0 to 1).
    pub albedo: vec3,
    /// The light it gives off itself, the same every way (radiance, in the units the sky's light
    /// is in).
    pub emits: vec3,
}

/// One over π.
pub const ONE_OVER_PI: f32 = 0.31830988

impl Substance {
    /// How much of the light arriving from the way `from` it sends toward the way `to` (both unit,
    /// away from the surface), facing `n`: its scattering function (BRDF), a share of the light
    /// arriving per unit of solid angle. Even every way: its albedo over π.
    pub fn scatters(self, n: vec3, from: vec3, to: vec3) -> vec3 {
        self.albedo * ONE_OVER_PI
    }
}
